      <section class="detail-section faq-section" id="buyer">
        <p class="eyebrow">For the buyer • Para el comprador</p>
        <h2>Questions before and after purchasing</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>
              What do I do after the inspection report? / ¿Qué hago después del
              reporte?
            </summary>
            <p>
              Start with safety, active water, structure, electrical, HVAC,
              plumbing, roof, and crawl-space moisture. Ask the right specialist
              to define the scope; an inspection observation is not
              automatically a repair specification.
            </p>
            <p class="spanish">
              Empiece por seguridad, agua activa, estructura, electricidad,
              HVAC, plomería, techo y humedad. Un comentario del inspector no
              siempre define cómo se debe reparar.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              Estimate, quote, or allowance—which can I rely on?
            </summary>
            <p>
              An estimate is a planning range. A quote identifies the verified
              scope, inclusions, exclusions, price, expiration, and assumptions.
              An allowance reserves money for an item not fully selected or
              known.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Should I request a repair or a closing credit?</summary>
            <p>
              That is a negotiation decision for the parties and agents. First
              learn the scope, urgency, timing, permit needs, and uncertainty. A
              credit does not correct an unsafe or insurance-sensitive
              condition.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>
              When is an engineer or licensed specialist needed?
            </summary>
            <p>
              Use the professional requested by the report or required by law.
              Structure, pests, HVAC, electrical, plumbing, roofing,
              mold/asbestos, septic, well, chimney, and environmental concerns
              may require their own qualified specialists.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I verify a completed repair?</summary>
            <p>
              Compare it with the signed scope. Request invoices, photos,
              warranty terms, permits and inspections when applicable. A visual
              walkthrough cannot certify concealed work.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What should I check at final walkthrough?</summary>
            <p>
              Confirm agreed repairs, systems and included appliances; look for
              new leaks or damage; verify cleanup; and collect keys, manuals,
              receipts, permits, and warranties.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do warranties transfer to me?</summary>
            <p>
              Not always. Ask whether labor, manufacturer, roof, HVAC,
              water-heater, termite, or other warranties transfer and whether
              registration is required.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What if I discover a problem after closing?</summary>
            <p>
              Prevent more damage, photograph the condition, review documents
              and warranties, and contact the right professional. Treat water,
              electrical, gas, sewage, or structural danger as urgent.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="detail-section white faq-section" id="seller">
        <p class="eyebrow">For the seller • Para el vendedor</p>
        <h2>Questions before listing and closing</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>
              What should I repair before listing? / ¿Qué debo reparar antes de
              vender?
            </summary>
            <p>
              Prioritize leaks, safety, moisture, damaged wood, nonworking
              systems, trip hazards, broken doors/windows, roof or siding
              problems, and unfinished work. Then consider paint, flooring,
              lighting, cleaning, and curb appeal.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Should I get a contractor walkthrough?</summary>
            <p>
              It can identify visible priorities, build a budget, and reduce
              surprises. It does not replace a licensed home inspection,
              engineering evaluation, appraisal, or disclosure advice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Which improvements may help value?</summary>
            <p>
              Favor condition, function, cleanliness, neutral finishes, and
              repairs buyers can verify. No contractor can guarantee sale price
              or return; confirm market priorities with your Realtor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do repairs need permits?</summary>
            <p>
              Some structural, electrical, plumbing, mechanical, additions,
              decks, and other work may require permits or licensed trades.
              Requirements depend on location and scope.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What if hidden damage is found?</summary>
            <p>
              Pause affected work, document the condition, and obtain a written
              change order with the new scope, price, and schedule. Do not rely
              on a verbal price change.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What proof should I give the buyer?</summary>
            <p>
              Provide paid invoices, written warranties, photos, permit records,
              inspection approvals, model/serial information, and a clear
              statement of what was—and was not—repaired.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can repairs be paid at closing?</summary>
            <p>
              Only when the contractor, closing professionals, lender, and
              transaction documents allow it in writing. Confirm everything
              before authorizing work.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I avoid repair delays?</summary>
            <p>
              Start early, provide access, make selections promptly, approve the
              scope, order long-lead materials, confirm permits, and leave room
              for concealed conditions.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="detail-section faq-section" id="realtor">
        <p class="eyebrow">For the Realtor</p>
        <h2>Questions for managing repair decisions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>Where does SAFE fit after the home inspection?</summary>
            <p>
              SAFE organizes findings by urgency and trade, identifies what can
              be estimated from documents, and routes items needing direct
              specialist evaluation. SAFE does not replace the inspector.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What should I send for useful guidance?</summary>
            <p>
              Property information, representation side, closing and
              due-diligence dates, relevant report pages, photos, access notes,
              client priorities, and the exact decision needed.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How should report items be organized?</summary>
            <p>
              Use six groups: immediate safety, active water/moisture,
              structural or wood, major systems, maintenance/remaining life, and
              cosmetic/optional improvements.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can a photo produce a final quote?</summary>
            <p>
              Sometimes. Access, quantity, hidden damage, code, material choice,
              and connected systems often require an on-site review before a
              firm scope.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do we coordinate several contractors?</summary>
            <p>
              Use one issue list with trade owner, status, due date, scope,
              price, warranty, and required documents. Avoid vague requests that
              combine unrelated trades.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What should be verified before closing?</summary>
            <p>
              Scope completion, invoices, warranties, permits/inspections when
              applicable, photos, manufacturer registration, cleanup, and
              intentionally excluded or deferred items.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How should insurance questions be handled?</summary>
            <p>
              Coverage, premium, underwriting, and insurability belong with a
              licensed agent. SAFE can connect clients with Carolinas Coverage
              at <a href="tel:+17045953429">704-595-3429</a>.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What should I avoid promising?</summary>
            <p>
              Do not guarantee price, completion date, permit approval,
              insurability, code compliance, remaining life, return on
              investment, engineering outcome, or concealed conditions.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
